.TH MOUNT.INVFS 8 "2026-10" "InvariantFS" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mount.invfs \- mount an InvariantFS volume (mount(8) helper)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount.invfs
.I device dir
.RB [ \-o
.IR opts ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mount.invfs
is the
.BR mount (8)
helper for fstype
.BR invfs ,
so fstab entries and
.B "mount \-t invfs"
work without naming the daemon.
It translates to
.B "invf-fuse [-o opts] device dir"
and waits for the mountpoint to appear in
.I /proc/mounts
before reporting success.
.PP
.B \-o
options pass through to the daemon,
.B \-r
appends the
.B ro
option,
.B \-f
prints the daemon command without running it,
.B \-n
and
.B \-s
are accepted and ignored (FUSE mounts never touch mtab).
Unknown options fail the mount; they are not silently dropped.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
/dev/sda4    /      invfs  defaults          0 1
/dev/sdb1    /data  invfs  noautopack,ro     0 2
.EE
.PP
Device paths are used above because
.BR blkid (8)
does not know the InvariantFS superblock magic yet, so
.B UUID=
lookups do not resolve. The initramfs identifies volumes with
.B invf-fuse --probe-uuid
instead.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR invf-fuse (8),
.BR mount (8),
.BR fstab (5),
.BR invarifs (7)
